One sentence in that body overclaims a change (the old normalize_bare already normalised 4_096/0x10). Fixing before push:
  ╭─ Turn 52 ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
  ▶ $ cd /home/runner/work/yoyo-evolve/yoyo-evolve && python3 - <<… (13 lines) ✓ (633ms)

A key written under a [section] no longer overrides the top-level model, provider or base_url. It's committed as de85ac91 (Part of #1184). Build, clippy with -D warnings, the full cargo test run and tests/harness_logic.sh (336 passed, 0 failed) all pass.

The fix: parse_config_file now builds its map from the parsed TOML and keeps only the top-level keys. The new builder is top_level_scalars_from_table in src/config_toml_read.rs. Values that are tables (a [header], a dotted key or an inline table) are skipped. If a file isn't valid TOML, the map comes back empty instead of falling back to the old line-by-line guess. The normal loader already refuses such a file before it gets here.

Checked on the built binary (temp home and repo; -p pointed at a dead 127.0.0.1 port, never a real vendor):

| Config | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| model = "claude-a", then [mcp_servers.fs] with model = "claude-b" | config get model and the -p header both said claude-b | both say claude-a |
| [permissions] then provider = "openai" | provider = openai | not set, plus a warning naming the line |

Three other places had to change too (found by listing everything that reads this map):
• Hooks. The hook reader expects keys like hooks.pre.bash, which TOML stores as a table, so skipping tables would have broken every hook. I flatten the hooks table instead. As a result, every way TOML can spell a hook now works, including [hooks.pre] and inline tables.
• Trust prompt. Because an inline-table hook now runs, the project-trust prompt also lists any hook found in the parsed config. Without that, such a hook could run after a trust answer that never mentioned hooks.
• Unknown-key warning. It used to treat model under any header as read, which pinned the old bug. It now warns and says to move the key above the first [section] header. My first binary probe caught a nonsense hint here ("did you mean provider?" about provider), which I fixed.

Tests: the new rows are in src/config_toplevel_tests.rs, and the five leak and hook-spelling rows failed on the old code. Disabling the table-skip turned the three leak rows red by name, and restoring it made them pass. The same check on the trust-prompt clause turned the inline-hook row red. The repo's own .yoyo.toml gives the same map with the old reader and the new one. I rewrote four existing tests that pinned the old behaviour and deleted none:
• two config.rs tests that used unquoted values, which TOML already rejects;
• one cd_config_note fixture that had a hook under [permissions], which TOML reads as a permissions key, not a hook;
• one unknown-key test, whose name is now a_top_level_name_under_a_section_is_named_because_it_is_not_read.

Behaviour changes you might notice:
• Hooks written as a table now run.
• A top-level array spread over several lines is now read; before, it was dropped.
• A float is stored as TOML writes it (1e3 becomes "1000.0"), which is the same number to every reader.

docs/src/getting-started/installation.md now states TOML's rule that top-level keys go before the first [section], and no longer says [hooks] is ignored.

Still open on #1184: the [permissions]/[directories] lists still come from the old line reader, config writes don't go through toml_edit yet, and the old parser isn't deleted. I posted a comment on #1184 with that list in its first 200 characters.

